uration: 400ms; --fade-animation-function: linear;"><p data-path-to-node=3D=\
"0">That is a great question. You are right to look closely at termination =\
clauses, as they directly impact your cap table and your ability to cut tie=\
s.</p><p data-path-to-node=3D"1">The short answer is: <b data-path-to-node=\
=3D"1" data-index-in-node=3D"21">Yes, this is completely normal, and it act=\
ually protects you.</b> Here is why it=E2=80=99s written this way and how i=\
t works in conjunction with the rest of the agreement:</p><h3 data-path-to-=\
node=3D"2">1. It <i data-path-to-node=3D"2" data-index-in-node=3D"6">is</i>=\
 "At-Will" (with a 30-day notice)</h3><p data-path-to-node=3D"3">If you loo=\
k just one paragraph up at <b data-path-to-node=3D"3" data-index-in-node=3D=\
"37">Section 4.2 (Cancellation)</b>, you will see:</p><blockquote data-path=\
-to-node=3D"4"><p data-path-to-node=3D"4,0"><i data-path-to-node=3D"4,0" da=\
ta-index-in-node=3D"0">"Consultant and/or Company may cancel this Agreement=\
 at any time without cause or reason with a thirty day notification in writ=\
ing..."</i></p></blockquote><p data-path-to-node=3D"5">This means the relat=\
ionship <i data-path-to-node=3D"5" data-index-in-node=3D"28">is</i> functio=\
nally "at-will." If things just aren't working out, or if Thad isn't provid=\
ing the value you hoped for, you don't need a legally actionable reason to =\
fire him. You just give him a 30-day notice, and the contract ends (and his=\
 equity stops vesting).</p><h3 data-path-to-node=3D"6">2. Why Section 4.3 (=\
"For Cause") is Necessary</h3><p data-path-to-node=3D"7">Section 4.3 is you=\
r <b data-path-to-node=3D"7" data-index-in-node=3D"20">"Emergency Ripcord."=\
</b> While Section 4.2 lets you fire him for <i data-path-to-node=3D"7" dat=\
a-index-in-node=3D"81">no</i> reason with a 30-day wait, Section 4.3 lets y=\
ou fire him <b data-path-to-node=3D"7" data-index-in-node=3D"141">immediate=\
ly, today, without waiting 30 days</b>, if he does something severely damag=\
ing to the company.</p><p data-path-to-node=3D"8">The contract specifically=\
 defines "Cause" as things like:</p><ul data-path-to-node=3D"9"><li><p data=\
-path-to-node=3D"9,0,0">Committing fraud or embezzlement.</p></li><li><p da=\
ta-path-to-node=3D"9,1,0">Being convicted of a felony.</p></li><li><p data-=\
path-to-node=3D"9,2,0">Breaching confidentiality (e.g., leaking OpenWorldTe=\
ch data).</p></li></ul><p data-path-to-node=3D"10"><b data-path-to-node=3D"=\
10" data-index-in-node=3D"0">Why this protects you:</b> If Thad does someth=\
ing illegal or breaches confidentiality, you do <i data-path-to-node=3D"10"=\
 data-index-in-node=3D"90">not</i> want to give him a 30-day notice. During=\
 that 30-day window, he would technically still be an advisor, he might sti=\
ll have access to your materials, and his equity would continue to vest.</p=\
><p data-path-to-node=3D"11">By having the "For Cause" clause, you have the=\
 legal right to sever ties instantly, freezing his equity vesting the secon=\
d you hand him the notice.</p><h3 data-path-to-node=3D"12">The Verdict</h3>=\
<p data-path-to-node=3D"13">It is incredibly standard for executive and adv=\
isory contracts to have <b data-path-to-node=3D"13" data-index-in-node=3D"7=\
1">both</b>. You have the flexibility to end things amicably with notice (4=\
.2), and you have the immediate hammer if things go horribly wrong (4.3).</=\
p><p data-path-to-node=3D"14">It looks good exactly as it is written! Are y=\
ou comfortable sending this revised V2 back to him now?</p></div></message-=\
